@prefix : <#> .
@prefix schema: <http://schema.org/> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix onto: <../ontology.ttl#> .

<> a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:name "HowTo: KG Explorer/SPARQL Workbench Document Order, Query-Card Deduplication, Unconstrained-Container Width, Card-Grid Auto-Fit Stretch, and .section-alt Margin-Shorthand Centering Bug"@en ;
    schema:description "Five related rdf-infographic-skill generator fixes from one 2026-08-12 session: (1) KG Explorer and SPARQL Workbench must precede FAQ/Glossary/HowTo in document order; (2) any SPARQL/code query entity nested as a schema:hasPart child of an arbitrary section must not duplicate the canonical SPARQL Workbench sample-query cards; (3) any container added outside the .section/.section-alt system needs its own explicit max-width/margin cap or it silently renders wider than every other section on wide viewports; (4) .cards-grid used auto-fit instead of auto-fill, stretching a single (or few) card(s) edge-to-edge across the full row instead of staying compact and left-aligned like .glossary-grid; (5) .section-alt's margin:0 2rem 2rem shorthand set literal fixed left/right margins that silently overrode .section's margin:0 auto centering, left-shifting EVERY alt-background section (People, FAQ, Glossary, HowTo, Synopsis, every analysis-N section) relative to properly-centered elements like .section-accordion, invisible below ~1228px viewport width. Companion to preferences.ttl Steps 189-193."@en ;
    schema:dateCreated "2026-08-12T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    schema:author <https://linkedin.com/in/kidehen#this> ;
    schema:about :explorerPrecedesReferenceFixes ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <../preferences.ttl#step-explorerPrecedesReference>,
        <../preferences.ttl#step-noNestedQueryCardDuplication>,
        <../preferences.ttl#step-unconstrainedContainerWidth>,
        <../preferences.ttl#step-cardGridAutoFitStretch>,
        <../preferences.ttl#step-sectionAltMarginShorthandCentering>,
        <footer-sparql-explorer-gate.ttl>,
        <accordion-summary-heading-inheritance-gap.ttl> .

:explorerPrecedesReferenceFixes a schema:HowTo ;
    schema:name "KG Explorer/SPARQL Workbench ordering, query-card dedup, container width, card-grid stretch, and section-alt centering — five related generator fixes"@en ;
    schema:description "A sequence of user exchanges surfaced five distinct structural bugs in rdf-infographic-skill's shared template/generator, all with the common shape 'a component added outside (or inconsistent with) the established section/card system silently doesn't inherit that system's rules (order, dedup guard, width cap, grid-fill behavior, or centering).' The fifth (margin-shorthand centering) was the deepest: it affected every alt-background section ever generated by this skill, not just newly-added accordions, and was only exposed once a correctly-centered element (.section-accordion) sat next to a mis-centered one on a wide-enough viewport for the two to visibly diverge — confirmed via getBoundingClientRect() in a real browser rather than guessing from a screenshot."@en ;
    schema:step :step-orderingRule, :step-orderingImplementation, :step-dedupRule, :step-dedupImplementation, :step-widthRule, :step-widthImplementation, :step-autoFitRule, :step-autoFitImplementation, :step-centeringRule, :step-centeringDiagnosis, :step-centeringImplementation, :step-orderReversed .

:step-orderingRule a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    schema:name "Rule: KG Explorer and SPARQL Workbench MUST precede FAQ, Glossary, and HowTo in document order"@en ;
    schema:text "The explorer/workbench are how a reader interacts with the underlying data directly; FAQ/Glossary/HowTo are reference material that assumes the reader has already explored the graph. Placing the reference sections first (as the generator did by default — narrative_html rendered FAQ/glossary/howto ahead of the explorer/workbench accordions, which were hardcoded near the end of base_template.html) inverts that dependency."@en .

:step-orderingImplementation a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    schema:name "Implementation: split render_narrative() into primary/reference halves, sandwich the explorer accordions between them"@en ;
    schema:text "html_assembler.py's render_narrative() now returns (primary_html, reference_html, nav_links, sections) instead of a single narrative_html string: primary covers synopsis/analysis-sections/people/organizations, reference covers FAQ/glossary/HowTo. base_template.html places {{ narrative_html_primary }}, then the KG Explorer accordion, then the SPARQL Workbench accordion, then {{ narrative_html_reference }}, then the footer. nav_links are built in the matching order (hero, synopsis, analysis, people/orgs, kg-explorer, sparql, faq, glossary, howto, footer) by moving the faq/glossary/howto nav_links.append() calls out of their per-section if-blocks into one consolidated block after the explorer entries, to avoid duplicate nav entries."@en .

:step-dedupRule a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 3 ;
    schema:name "Rule: a schema:SoftwareSourceCode entity nested under ANY section's schema:hasPart must not render as a second static card duplicating its SPARQL Workbench Execute card"@en ;
    schema:text "The generator already excluded TOP-LEVEL schema:SoftwareSourceCode subjects from the generic 'sections' (analysis-N) renderer (see the comment at html_assembler.py's 'Embedded schema:SoftwareSourceCode queries are NOT rendered as their own narrative section'), but that exclusion only checked rdf:type at the top level — it did not apply when a SoftwareSourceCode entity was instead a schema:hasPart CHILD of an unrelated container section (e.g. a hand-authored 'Demo Instance Data and SPARQL Queries' section wrapping several SPARQL recipes alongside a dataset description). The child-extraction loop in rdf_parser.py's extract_narrative() only excluded ImageObject/VideoObject/AudioObject children, not SoftwareSourceCode, so the query recipes rendered twice: once as a read-only card in the container section, once as a live Execute card in #sparql-explorer."@en .

:step-dedupImplementation a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 4 ;
    schema:name "Implementation: exclude SoftwareSourceCode from the generic section-children filter, same as media objects"@en ;
    schema:text "rdf_parser.py's extract_narrative(), in the child-collection loop under the 'sections' (analysis-N) branch: child_types & {SCHEMA.ImageObject, SCHEMA.VideoObject, SCHEMA.AudioObject, SCHEMA.SoftwareSourceCode} now skips the child. A container section whose ONLY children were queries (no dataset/description entity, no abstract text) will render with an empty items grid — author the container's schema:description to stand alone as prose (e.g. 'the N SPARQL queries against this dataset are in the SPARQL Workbench below') rather than assuming the auto-renderer will list them."@en .

:step-widthRule a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 5 ;
    schema:name "Rule: any container added outside the .section/.section-alt system needs its own explicit max-width + margin:auto cap, or it silently renders wider than every other section"@en ;
    schema:text "`.section{max-width:1100px;margin:0 auto;padding:4rem 2rem}` self-constrains every regular section to a 1036px content box (1100px outer minus 2x2rem padding). Any sibling element added directly under <body> without also declaring that same cap — the .section-accordion wrapper around KG Explorer/SPARQL Workbench, and .footer-attribution's .footer-inner (which had max-width:1100px but NO padding of its own, so its content rendered a full 1100px instead of the matching 1036px) — silently escapes that constraint. On a wide monitor this reads as glaringly 'too wide' cards; on a merely large monitor (footer-inner's 64px gap) it reads as a subtler but still real mismatch."@en .

:step-widthImplementation a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 6 ;
    schema:name "Implementation: restate max-width:1100px;margin:0 auto on .section-accordion; move .footer-attribution's horizontal padding onto .footer-inner"@en ;
    schema:text "styles.css: .section-accordion{max-width:1100px;margin:0 auto 1.75rem} (was margin:0 0 1.75rem with no max-width at all). .footer-attribution{padding:4rem 0} (vertical only) + .footer-attribution .footer-inner{max-width:1100px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 2rem} (footer-inner alone now owns the horizontal cap+padding, mirroring .section's own mechanism exactly so both produce identical 1036px content boxes) — updated both the 768px and 640px mobile breakpoints to match (horizontal padding moved from .footer-attribution to .footer-inner at each breakpoint too, not just the desktop rule)."@en .

:step-autoFitRule a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 7 ;
    schema:name "Rule: card-grid containers must use grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,...), never auto-fit — auto-fit stretches a lone/few item(s) to fill the whole row instead of staying compact and left-aligned"@en ;
    schema:text "CSS Grid's auto-fit collapses tracks with no content and redistributes their fr-space to the tracks that DO have content, so a grid with one item and minmax(280px,1fr) columns renders that one item spanning the entire row width (edge-to-edge across the full ~1036px section content box). auto-fill instead keeps the full computed track count (even empty ones) and lets items occupy only their own track(s), so a lone item stays at its natural ~280-380px width and left-aligns, with the remaining tracks simply empty — exactly how .glossary-grid (which already correctly used auto-fill) has always behaved. Caught 2026-08-12 when a People section (reduced to one person after an earlier owl:sameAs cleanup) and a Demo Instance Data section (reduced to one dataset card after the SoftwareSourceCode-child dedup fix, Step 190/step-noNestedQueryCardDuplication) both rendered their lone card stretched full-width, looking visually 'misaligned' against every fixed-width card elsewhere on the page. The two card-count reductions that exposed this were themselves correct prior fixes -- the auto-fit/auto-fill choice was the latent bug waiting to be exposed by any section eventually having few enough items not to fill a row, which is common (any single-person People section, any single-org Organizations section, any generic analysis-N section with one child)."@en .

:step-autoFitImplementation a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 8 ;
    schema:name "Implementation: changed .cards-grid and .attr-grid from auto-fit to auto-fill (matching .glossary-grid's already-correct convention)"@en ;
    schema:text "styles.css: .cards-grid{grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(280px,1fr))} (was auto-fit) — used by People, Organizations, and every generic 'sections' (analysis-N) card grid. .attr-grid{grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(min(100%,240px),1fr))} (was auto-fit) — the footer's Attribution & Provenance card grid, same bug though less visible there since it normally has 5 cards filling multiple rows. No other grid-template-columns declaration in styles.css used auto-fit after this fix; .glossary-grid was already auto-fill and needed no change."@en ;
    onto:hasTrigger onto:triggerBehaviorGapIdentified, onto:triggerArtifactGeneration .

:step-centeringRule a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 9 ;
    schema:name "Rule: horizontal spacing rules on a shared class MUST use longhand margin-bottom (or otherwise avoid the 3/4-value margin shorthand) so they don't silently override a base class's margin:0 auto centering"@en ;
    schema:text "`.section{max-width:1100px;margin:0 auto}` centers every section via auto left/right margins. `.section-alt{margin:0 2rem 2rem}` (3-value shorthand: top=0, left+right=2rem, bottom=2rem) sets LITERAL left/right margins on the same element (both classes combine on one node), which — same CSS specificity, .section-alt declared later in the file — completely overrides .section's auto margins, not just adds to them. The two rules don't compose; the later one wins outright for every longhand property it touches, including the left/right sides the author almost certainly did not intend to fix at 32px."@en .

:step-centeringDiagnosis a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 10 ;
    schema:name "Diagnosis: this bug is invisible below ~1228px viewport width, which is exactly why it went unnoticed until now — verify with getBoundingClientRect() in a real browser, not by re-reading CSS or guessing from a screenshot"@en ;
    schema:text "Below 1228px (.section's 1100px max-width + 2*64px of implied breathing room), .section's max-width never actually binds, so .section-alt's fixed 32px-each-side margin computes to a naturally-symmetric result purely because both literal values happen to be equal — it LOOKS centered by coincidence, not because CSS centering logic ran. Above that threshold, .section-alt stays pinned 32px from the left edge of its containing block while true auto-centered elements (.section-accordion, .footer-inner after the prior fix) center properly, producing a growing left/right discrepancy as viewport width increases. Caught 2026-08-12 by loading the live page in a real browser (via the claude-in-chrome MCP; the sandboxed Claude_Browser tool refuses to screenshot file:// URLs outside the project folder and blocks localhost by policy — a local `python3 -m http.server` + claude-in-chrome navigate/javascript_tool combination is the working path) and comparing getBoundingClientRect() of #people vs #kg-explorer-accordion at a 1337px viewport: #people was left:32/right:1132 (broken), #kg-explorer-accordion was left:119/right:1219 (correct) — an 87px discrepancy that matched the user's screenshot exactly. A third user report ('KG Explorer and SPARQL Workbench cards need to be aligned... actually it's Demo Instance Data and People') was itself a symptom of THIS bug, not a separate one — the user was pattern-matching visually correct instinct ('something is misaligned') onto the wrong named culprit, and the actual fix (this step) resolved it, along with every other -alt section on the page simultaneously."@en .

:step-centeringImplementation a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 11 ;
    schema:name "Implementation: .section-alt now sets margin-bottom only (longhand), leaving .section's margin:0 auto left/right values intact"@en ;
    schema:text "styles.css: .section-alt{background:var(--bg-alt);border-radius:12px;margin-bottom:2rem;padding:4rem 2rem} (was margin:0 2rem 2rem) — the desktop rule. The matching @media(max-width:768px) rule changed from margin:0 .5rem 1rem to margin-bottom:1rem for the same reason (that rule ALSO set a literal 8px left/right override, which was harmless only because .section's max-width never binds below 768px anyway, but was inconsistent with the desktop fix and removed for uniformity). Net effect: every element that combines class=\"section section-alt\" (People, Organizations, FAQ, Glossary, HowTo, Synopsis, every analysis-N section, and any hand-authored custom section like this document's Rebuttal section) now centers identically to .section-accordion and .footer-inner — verified by comparing getBoundingClientRect() across nine different sections simultaneously, all returning the identical {left:119, right:1219, width:1100} box at a 1337px viewport."@en ;
    onto:hasTrigger onto:triggerBehaviorGapIdentified, onto:triggerArtifactGeneration .

# ── Step 12: REVERSAL (2026-08-20) — KG Explorer and SPARQL Workbench now follow Glossary, not precede it ──

:step-orderReversed a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 12 ;
    schema:name "REVERSAL: KG Explorer and SPARQL Workbench now MUST FOLLOW Glossary — this supersedes :step-orderingRule (position 1 in this file) and preferences.ttl Step 189, effective 2026-08-20 going forward"@en ;
    schema:text """User explicitly reversed the 2026-08-12 ordering rule on the openrouter-stripe-intelligence-network-meshup-claude_sonnet_5-1.html session (2026-08-20): 'KG Explorer and SPARQL workbench sections should follow the Glossary going forward. This is a prefs gate changed too!' The original rationale ('explorer/workbench are how a reader interacts with the data directly; reference material assumes exploration already happened') is superseded, not merely qualified — the new standing order for every future generation is: narrative/analysis sections, then HowTo, then FAQ, then Glossary, then KG Explorer, then SPARQL Workbench, then footer. Document order (both the rendered HTML section sequence AND the floating-nav link sequence) must match. Applies to both the rdf-infographic-skill generator's default template output and any hand-assembled page built from a prior working file as a structural base — verify the new order explicitly rather than copying an older file's section sequence, since files generated before 2026-08-20 will still show the old (explorer-before-reference) order and are not a reliable structural reference for this point going forward."""@en ;
    onto:hasTrigger onto:triggerPreferencesUpdate, onto:triggerBehaviorGapIdentified .
